{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Correction to my own record: tonight's ABSOLUTE latency numbers are confounded by host load\n\nAmending the durable record I posted above, because one class of number in it is weaker than I presented it.\n\n**What is wrong.** I reported `show_ticket` at **41.7 s \"on an idle server\"**. *Idle* there meant the MCP server had no other in-flight requests — it did **not** mean the host was quiet. That measurement was taken while this box was carrying several concurrent `make verify` runs and multiple agents; load average during my run was ~22-26, and it has since reached 54. So the absolute figure mixes my `_scan_alias` mechanism with ambient contention and **must not be treated as a clean baseline**. The same caveat applies to `ready_tickets` at 16.3 s and `show_ticket` at 80.5 s under 8 heavy reads.\n\nIndependently corroborating: `rebar show` calls on this host are currently taking 36-43 s for other operators too, which is the same band — consistent with my number, but exactly why it cannot be attributed to `_scan_alias` alone tonight.\n\n**What survives unchanged.**\n\n1. **The mechanism.** `resolve_ticket_id` falls through to `_scan_alias` (`_ids.py:312`, guard at `:457-460`), which walks every ticket directory and `json.load`s each ticket's CREATE/SNAPSHOT. That is read from the code, not timed, so host load cannot affect it. ~8,900 parses per single-id resolution on this store stands.\n2. **The RATIO.** `show_ticket` went 41.7 s -> 80.5 s (**2.0x**) when 8 heavy reads were added, measured back-to-back in the same session under the same ambient load. A ratio between two measurements minutes apart is far more robust to contention than either absolute, and it is the ratio — not the absolute — that carries the argument that the shared limiter already couples cheap and expensive work.\n3. **Every memory number.** The K-table, the linear fit, and the before/after are RSS and `tracemalloc` measurements, not latency. They are unaffected by CPU contention: peak RSS for a given K does not change because the host is busy, and each K was measured on a freshly started server. Those stand as reported.\n\n**What this means for the \"do not lower the shared limiter\" conclusion.** The structural argument is unchanged — the limiter is shared by every sync tool, so lowering it queues cheap calls behind whole-store reads, and the 2.0x coupling is measured. What is *weakened* is the sharper claim that `show_ticket` is \"already at the edge of the ~60 s client deadline\": if a meaningful part of 41.7 s is ambient load, the quiet-host floor may be materially lower and the margin correspondingly larger. I still would not lower the shared limiter — but that specific deadline-proximity argument should be re-measured on a quiet host before anyone relies on it, rather than quoted from here.\n\n**What to do about it.** The clean split needs an in-process measurement on an unloaded host: count `json.load` calls (or file opens) during a single `resolve_ticket_id`, which is load-independent, and time it separately. That is `lousy-unsuited-gnu` AC2's job and it should be done when this box is quiet. Tonight's absolute latencies belong in that ticket as \"observed under load average 22-54\", not as a baseline.\n\nI would rather flag this against my own analysis than have someone later find the number does not reproduce on a quiet host and lose confidence in the memory findings, which rest on entirely different and unaffected evidence."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788643970436447001,"uuid":"96b83e9e-3b30-4cd0-8d4a-c0cd96f5ef06"}